<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/expert/international-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    International law governs <strong>relations between sovereign states — law without a
    supreme enforcer</strong>. Its puzzle: why do states obey at all? The answer (reciprocity,
    reputation, domestic institutions, shared interest in order) explains both its successes
    (trade, treaties, human rights) and its failures (enforcement against great powers).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the sources, the courts, and the compliance mystery" widget="equationStep" config={intlCfg} />

  <h2>The major regimes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Human rights law:</strong> UDHR (1948) → binding covenants (1966) → regional courts (European Court of Human Rights — the most effective system, with individual access).</li>
    <li><strong>Law of armed conflict:</strong> Geneva Conventions (1949) — the rules even war must follow; the ICC (2002) prosecutes individuals for genocide, war crimes, crimes against humanity.</li>
    <li><strong>Trade and sea:</strong> WTO dispute settlement (binding, enforced through authorised retaliation), UNCLOS (the "constitution for the oceans").</li>
    <li><strong>Critiques worth knowing:</strong> sovereignty vs intervention (UN Security Council veto politics), treaty withdrawal risks, and the "is international law really law?" question — answered best by Austin's critics: it functions as law among its subjects, which is what law does.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Vienna Convention (1969); Shaw, International Law; Henkin, How Nations Behave.</p>
</TopicPage>
